## Step 3: Assess Pareto Front Candidates

The most interesting Pareto-front region is **high PF (≥0.35) with reasonable E (≥400)**. Looking at the data:

- **17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2-1800s**: E=520, PF=0.36 — single run, wrinkles present, but this is the best single-run combination of high E + high PF among quality-acceptable runs. Needs validation.
- **19-0add-10deg-240s-NoN2-1600s**: run1 E=516/PF=0.33 (wrinkle), run2 E=309/PF=0.37 (good quality). Mean ~413/0.35 — inconsistent, high uncertainty.
- **19p5-0add-25deg-130s-NoN2-1550s**: run1 E=351/PF=0.43 (minor wrinkle), run4 E=570/PF=0.21 — wildly inconsistent, runs 2&3 missing modulus. Not reliable.

The **17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2-1800s** condition (mixing_temp=65, bath_temp=15, pullcast_speed=10, nitrogen=True, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=60, nips_bath_wait_time=1800, polymer_wt=17.5) is the most promising unvalidated Pareto candidate: E=520 bar, PF=0.36, with only one run and wrinkle defects noted. It needs an independent repeat to confirm whether this combination is reproducible and whether the wrinkles are systematic.

However, I should also consider whether there's a better **unexplored** region. The data suggests:
- Lower bath temp (5°C) + moderate polymer wt (17-18%) + N2 + moderate wait times tend to give higher E but lower PF (~0.27-0.33)
- Higher bath temp (15-25°C) + lower polymer wt tends to give higher PF but variable E
- The 17.5% / bath_temp=15°C / N2 / short wait combination gave E=520, PF=0.36 in one run

**Validation of 17p5-0add-15deg-60s-N2-1800s is the priority** — it's the best single-run candidate combining E≥500 and PF≥0.35 with good membrane quality context (despite wrinkles, the mechanical data was obtained). An independent repeat will clarify whether this is a true Pareto improvement.

