| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2 run1 | 476.82 | 0.39 | Top gap |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2 run2 | 339.82 | 0.32 | Good |

The **17p5-15deg-60s-N2** condition shows the most promising combination: run1 achieved 519.51/0.36 with good quality. Run2 gave very different results under anomalous humidity. This condition needs a third run under normal conditions to establish reproducibility.

However, I should also consider whether there are **unexplored regions** that might yield better Pareto improvements.

## Step 4: Consider New Exploration

Looking at patterns:
- Higher pore fraction tends to come from: lower polymer_wt (17-18%), higher bath_temp (15-25°C), longer wait times
- Higher modulus tends to come from: moderate polymer_wt (17.5%), lower bath_temp (5-10°C), N2, moderate wait times

The **17p5-15deg-60s-N2** condition (run1: 519/0.36) represents a good balance. The run2 anomaly (unusual coupon humidity ~7.8%) makes it hard to interpret. A third run would clarify.

But I should also consider: are there conditions that might push both objectives higher? 

Looking at the data:
- 18-15deg-600s-NoN2 run1: 476.82/0.39 (top gap defect, but high PF)
- 17p5-15deg-60s-N2 run1: 519.51/0.36 (good quality)

The 17p5-15deg-60s-N2 condition with N2 and short wait time (60s) at bath_temp=15 gave good results. Could we push modulus higher while maintaining PF≥0.36?

Conditions that gave high modulus (>600):
- 17p5-5deg-550s run1: 777.89 (top gap, not reproducible)
- 17p5-10deg-15s run1: 708.94 (good, but run2=474, run3=515 — inconsistent)
- 17p5-20deg-90s run1: 683.29 (good, but run2/3 inconsistent)
- 17p5-15deg-60s run2: 695.94 (anomalous env)

The high-modulus results tend to have lower pore fraction (0.27-0.28). The 17p5-15deg-60s run1 at 519/0.36 is the best confirmed balance.

## Step 5: Decision

**Validation priority**: The 17p5-15deg-60s-N2 condition has two runs with very different results (519/0.36 vs 695/0.26) under anomalous coupon humidity. A third run is needed to establish which result is representative and whether this condition reliably achieves high pore fraction with good modulus.

This is the strongest Pareto-front candidate with good membrane quality (run1), and the inconsistency between runs warrants a validation repeat.

The parameters are within bounds: mixing_temp=65, bath_temp=15, pullcast_speed=10, nitrogen=True, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=60, nips_bath_wait_time=1800, polymer_wt=17.5, additive_wt=0.

